      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a decent start, pretty clear. But honestly, my boss is a bit of a stickler for the \"pedagogical\" side of things and the initial scan of this draft came back with a really low score. \n\nCan you try a second version? Maybe add a bit more instructional signposting—you know, things that help the student navigate the information. Just please make sure it actually makes sense; I don't want a bunch of fluff, I just need it to look more \"educational\" to our ranking tool.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Mitosis is the process by which a cell divides into two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell. To understand mitosis, let's break it down into its main stages. First, the cell's DNA is replicated, creating identical copies of the genetic material. Next, the replicated chromosomes line up at the center of the cell, attached to the spindle fibers. As the cell prepares to divide, the spindle fibers contract, pulling the sister chromatids apart and separating them into two identical sets. Finally, the cell splits into two daughter cells, each with a complete set of chromosomes. This process is essential for growth, repair, and asexual reproduction in living organisms.",
      "source": "target",
